{"claims":[{"excerpt":"### Yellowstonewolven in een web van sociale en voedselrelaties","claim":"Yellowstone wolves are part of social and food relationships.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The pack is a complex social family, with older members (often the alpha male and alpha female) and subordinates","narrative":"Yellowstonewolven zijn sociale roofdieren: ze jagen in groepen en hun prooien voeden ook andere dieren.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The title aptly summarizes the source’s descriptions of pack social life and the food relationships created by wolf kills.","materiality":"This frames the piece’s central point."},{"excerpt":"Yellowstonewolven leven in sociale familiegroepen.","claim":"Yellowstone wolves live in social family groups.","category":"entity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The pack is a complex social family, with older members (often the alpha male and alpha female) and subordinates","narrative":"Introduce Yellowstonewolven als sociale familiegroepen, met onder meer gezamenlijke jacht en zorg voor jongen.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"This accurately summarizes the source’s description of wolf packs as complex social families.","materiality":"Provides context for the wolves’ social behavior."},{"excerpt":"Ze jagen samen en zorgen voor hun jongen.","claim":"Yellowstone wolves hunt together and cooperatively care for offspring.","category":"entity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Research in Yellowstone since reintroduction has highlighted the adaptive value of social living in wolves – from cooperative care of offspring, group hunting of large prey","narrative":"Introduce Yellowstonewolven als sociale familiegroepen, met onder meer gezamenlijke jacht en zorg voor jongen.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"Both behaviors are explicitly identified in the source.","materiality":"Supports the description of wolves as social animals."},{"excerpt":"Ook na een wolvendoding krijgen andere dieren voedsel: van raven en coyotes tot beren en ongewervelden.","claim":"Other animals, including ravens, coyotes, bears, and invertebrates, obtain food from wolf kills.","category":"entity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Many other animals benefit from wolf kills. For example, when wolves kill an elk, ravens and magpies arrive almost immediately. Coyotes arrive soon after, waiting nearby until the wolves are sated. Bears will attempt to chase the wolves away, and are usually successful. Many other animals—from eagles to invertebrates—consume the remains.","narrative":"Laat zien dat een wolvendoding voedsel biedt aan dieren van raven en coyotes tot beren en ongewervelden.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source lists these animals as benefiting from or consuming the remains of wolf kills.","materiality":"Establishes the food-relationship example central to the piece."},{"excerpt":"De prooien van wolven veranderden in de loop der tijd.","claim":"The composition of wolves’ prey changed over time.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"From 1995 to 2000, in early winter, elk calves comprised 50% of wolf prey, and bull elk comprised 25%. That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007","narrative":"De veranderde samenstelling van hun prooi in verschillende perioden maakt concreet dat predatiepatronen veranderen binnen omstandigheden waarin meerdere factoren meespelen.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source documents a change in the composition of early-winter prey across the stated periods.","materiality":"Introduces the specific prey-composition example."},{"excerpt":"Onder de prooien die in de vroege winter werden gedood, verschoof de verhouding tussen elandkalveren en volwassen mannelijke elanden tussen 1995–2000 en 2001–2007.","claim":"Among early-winter wolf prey, the relative proportions of elk calves and bull elk shifted between 1995–2000 and 2001–2007.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"From 1995 to 2000, in early winter, elk calves comprised 50% of wolf prey, and bull elk comprised 25%. That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007","narrative":"Gebruik daarnaast de verschuiving in de verhouding tussen elandkalveren en volwassen mannelijke elanden onder de vroege-winterprooien in 1995–2000 en 2001–2007 als concreet voorbeeld van veranderende prooisamenstelling","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source says the ratio reversed across these periods; the piece accurately reports a shift without adding unsupported figures.","materiality":"Provides the piece’s concrete evidence of changing prey composition."},{"excerpt":"Die veranderingen worden in verband gebracht met de beschikbaarheid en kwetsbaarheid van prooien.","claim":"The prey-composition changes are associated with prey availability and vulnerability.","category":"cause","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007, indicating changes in prey vulnerability and availability.","narrative":"de bron verbindt die veranderingen met de beschikbaarheid en kwetsbaarheid van prooien.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source says the ratio reversal indicates changes in vulnerability and availability; the piece’s wording conveys an association rather than asserting a single cause.","materiality":"Qualifies how the prey shift is interpreted."},{"excerpt":"De veranderende predatiepatronen en effecten op elanden vallen samen met andere factoren, waaronder andere roofdieren, het beheer van hoefdieren buiten het park en het weer.","claim":"Changes in wolf predation patterns and effects on elk occur in a context involving other predators, ungulate management outside the park, and weather.","category":"cause","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Changes in wolf predation patterns and impacts on prey species like elk are inextricably linked to other factors, such as other predators, management of ungulates outside the park, and weather (e.g. drought, winter severity).","narrative":"veranderingen in predatie en de effecten op elanden samenvallen met andere factoren, waaronder andere roofdieren, beheer van hoefdieren buiten het park en het weer.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source explicitly identifies these factors as linked to changes in predation patterns and impacts on prey. The piece does not claim that wolves alone cause the observed outcomes.","materiality":"Preserves the source’s multi-factor account of changing predation and elk impacts."},{"excerpt":"Samen laten deze observaties zien dat wolven deel uitmaken van sociale en voedselrelaties in Yellowstone, binnen een ecosysteem waarin meerdere factoren meespelen.","claim":"The observations show that Yellowstone wolves participate in social and food relationships within an ecosystem shaped by multiple factors.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The pack is a complex social family","narrative":"Zo blijft het verhaal bij een complex samenspel, niet bij wolven als enige bepalende kracht.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"This is a restrained synthesis of the source’s descriptions of pack sociality, wolf kills feeding other animals, and multiple factors linked to predation and elk impacts.","materiality":"Summarizes the piece’s conclusion without claiming wolves are the sole ecosystem driver."}]}

